WEBVTT

welcome-1
00:00:00.554 --> 00:00:07.559
ccdash is a dashboard for viewing Claude Code and Codex usage, costs, and workflows
together.

welcome-2
00:00:07.599 --> 00:00:14.521
We’ll start with how to read the opening screen, then follow user activity, model calls,
and tool usage.

welcome-3
00:00:14.561 --> 00:00:18.521
The values on screen are example data for explaining the features.

architecture-1
00:00:19.970 --> 00:00:28.450
In this example setup, a local Collector gathers telemetry and stores it in ClickHouse
through an authenticated ingestion path.

architecture-2
00:00:28.490 --> 00:00:33.500
The browser displays results queried by the Express API.

architecture-3
00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:37.975
Browser sign-in and device ingestion credentials are separate.

architecture-4
00:00:38.015 --> 00:00:43.889
The two Express boxes in the diagram represent logical roles within the same
application.

language-1
00:00:45.345 --> 00:00:48.825
Choose from five languages at the bottom of the sidebar.

language-2
00:00:48.865 --> 00:00:51.875
On mobile, open the menu to make your selection.

language-3
00:00:51.915 --> 00:00:57.562
Your selection persists across visits, and your open work and query period stay as they
are.

language-4
00:00:57.602 --> 00:01:00.864
The guide also opens in the same language.

window-1
00:01:02.179 --> 00:01:07.471
Before comparing, align the client, backend, and supported user and model filters.

window-2
00:01:07.511 --> 00:01:13.546
The date range lets you enter start and end times to the second, as well as dates.

window-3
00:01:13.586 --> 00:01:19.309
Time fields use your browser’s time zone; the date picker uses the UTC calendar.

window-4
00:01:19.349 --> 00:01:26.391
Share links preserve the same interval, but check the server’s aggregation interval and
ingestion delay separately.

overview-1
00:01:27.845 --> 00:01:32.600
In Overview, start with costs, observed tokens, sessions, and users.

overview-2
00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:36.675
The trend below is a starting point for finding busy periods.

overview-3
00:01:36.715 --> 00:01:41.012
Read the cost basis and partial-total indicators beside the totals.

overview-4
00:01:41.052 --> 00:01:46.964
Unavailable data is not zero, and the presence of values alone does not mean collection
is complete.

executive-1
00:01:48.429 --> 00:01:53.571
Use Executive for a quick view of overall usage and cost composition.

executive-2
00:01:53.611 --> 00:01:59.046
After aligning the period and collection scope, compare the client and model mix.

executive-3
00:01:59.086 --> 00:02:02.871
Channels in Claude details are distinctions inferred from sessions.

executive-4
00:02:02.911 --> 00:02:06.804
They do not represent experiment groups or individual performance.

trends-1
00:02:08.187 --> 00:02:14.492
In Trends, compare with the previous period to see when tokens, costs, and activity
changed.

trends-2
00:02:14.532 --> 00:02:23.541
When you spot a spike, narrow to a shorter interval and open Users or Cost with the same
filters to investigate.

users-1
00:02:24.958 --> 00:02:31.612
Select a user row in Users to see that user’s totals, usage trend, and activity periods.

users-2
00:02:31.652 --> 00:02:38.887
Finding users with high costs is a starting point for investigating work, not evaluating
performance.

users-3
00:02:38.927 --> 00:02:44.017
Distinguish the masked display from actual API access permissions.

range-1
00:02:45.470 --> 00:02:51.637
Drag across the usage and cost charts in user details to narrow to the time range you
want.

range-2
00:02:51.677 --> 00:02:58.362
User details stay open, while the top 20 work items update to that period.

range-3
00:02:58.402 --> 00:03:01.625
Select a bucket by clicking a point.

range-4
00:03:01.665 --> 00:03:05.909
Check the model and workflow labels, then open the work you want to inspect.

trace-1
00:03:07.333 --> 00:03:13.225
In Trace details, review total tokens, cost, and call count as you follow the timeline.

trace-2
00:03:13.265 --> 00:03:18.900
Even when Explore types match, separate agents appear in separate lanes.

trace-3
00:03:18.940 --> 00:03:24.125
Distinguish model calls, tool executions, and periods spent waiting for user approval.

trace-4
00:03:24.165 --> 00:03:31.696
Use the cumulative cost chart and lanes together to find when and which agent
accumulated costs.

call-1
00:03:33.012 --> 00:03:40.129
Select a call bar to see the model, duration, input and output tokens, and cache tokens.

call-2
00:03:40.169 --> 00:03:45.067
The cost source indicates whether it was reported by the client or calculated as an
estimate.

call-3
00:03:45.107 --> 00:03:48.579
Sources can differ even within the same work item.

call-4
00:03:48.619 --> 00:03:53.292
TTFT and attempt details appear only when collected.

call-5
00:03:53.332 --> 00:03:57.320
Do not interpret blank values as zero or as meaning there were no retries.

context-1
00:03:58.687 --> 00:04:03.617
Select a point in the context graph to return to that call.

context-2
00:04:03.657 --> 00:04:07.879
The model calls table helps highlight related calls.

context-3
00:04:07.919 --> 00:04:15.817
In the session environment, check observed MCP connection status, loaded plugins, and
the context of the first call.

context-4
00:04:15.857 --> 00:04:21.889
Information that is not collected, such as the token size of each tool definition,
cannot be determined from this screen alone.

cost-1
00:04:23.270 --> 00:04:28.400
In Cost, compare how costs are distributed across models, users, and backends.

cost-2
00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:32.337
First check the basis for reported and estimated costs.

cost-3
00:04:32.377 --> 00:04:38.187
Claude reported values, AWS list-price estimates, and calculation diagnostics have
different meanings.

cost-4
00:04:38.227 --> 00:04:41.762
None of these values is an invoice or guarantees the actual bill.

cost-5
00:04:41.802 --> 00:04:48.416
Zero-cost items appear in a collapsed list; items with unknown costs are marked
unestimated.

economics-1
00:04:49.720 --> 00:04:57.600
Productivity’s cost equation links users, sessions, turns, requests, tokens, and unit
prices to explain total cost.

economics-2
00:04:57.640 --> 00:05:03.300
The driver breakdown shows which factors increased or decreased the amount from the
previous period.

economics-3
00:05:03.340 --> 00:05:08.225
Even when unit prices fall, total costs can rise if usage increases by more.

economics-4
00:05:08.265 --> 00:05:14.628
These are observed measures of activity and efficiency, not causal evidence of
investment impact or employee productivity assessments.

unit-1
00:05:16.095 --> 00:05:22.512
Compare cost per user, session, or amount of code only when the required denominator and
collection scope are available.

unit-2
00:05:22.552 --> 00:05:28.262
The dashed line in the unit-cost trend is a visual guide connecting unknown intervals
for readability.

unit-3
00:05:28.302 --> 00:05:32.070
Those intervals are not treated as measurements or calculated as zero.

usage-1
00:05:33.500 --> 00:05:40.117
In Usage, compare the mix of input, output, and cache tokens alongside tool usage.

usage-2
00:05:40.157 --> 00:05:46.942
Check whether cache and reasoning tokens are subsets of total usage to avoid double
counting.

usage-3
00:05:46.982 --> 00:05:51.095
Also look at changes in request counts and context size.

tools-1
00:05:52.479 --> 00:05:59.721
In the tools table in Claude details, compare executions, errors, and latency to find
recurring failures.

tools-2
00:05:59.761 --> 00:06:03.271
The permission decision table is different from tool execution results.

tools-3
00:06:03.311 --> 00:06:09.946
Approval does not mean execution succeeded; distinguish automatic approval from
requiring user approval each time.

tools-4
00:06:09.986 --> 00:06:16.545
If you find a problem period or user, return to the work Trace to inspect the actual
calls.

mcp-1
00:06:18.062 --> 00:06:25.217
In MCP, see which servers and connectors were used and compare their calls and success
rates.

mcp-2
00:06:25.257 --> 00:06:29.517
Connection status and actual usage are different kinds of information.

mcp-3
00:06:29.557 --> 00:06:34.875
If you see failures, also check that work’s tool executions and session environment.

skills-1
00:06:36.292 --> 00:06:40.746
In Skills, compare which skills were used and how they were invoked.

skills-2
00:06:40.786 --> 00:06:46.384
Distinguish direct calls, automatic activation, and continuation from another skill.

skills-3
00:06:46.424 --> 00:06:51.884
Costs attributed to a skill are not separate charges to add again to total costs.

skills-4
00:06:51.924 --> 00:06:56.215
Read this as telemetry for finding related sessions and work.

plugins-1
00:06:57.604 --> 00:07:02.121
The plugin list shows which extensions were loaded into a session.

plugins-2
00:07:02.161 --> 00:07:06.896
Being loaded does not by itself mean a feature was executed.

plugins-3
00:07:06.936 --> 00:07:15.694
Check the session environment along with model, agent, and command configuration to
investigate why the same work differs across environments.

reliability-1
00:07:17.062 --> 00:07:22.729
In Reliability, review errors, response times, versions, and cost diagnostics.

reliability-2
00:07:22.769 --> 00:07:27.492
Look at distributions and periods where problems cluster, not just a single average.

reliability-3
00:07:27.532 --> 00:07:34.288
When usage falls, first distinguish a real drop in activity from ingestion delays or
missing data.

analytics-1
00:07:35.770 --> 00:07:39.925
Analytics provides a closer look at models and diagnostic signals.

analytics-2
00:07:39.965 --> 00:07:45.162
Question-based analysis is also available when permissions and model settings are in
place.

analytics-3
00:07:45.202 --> 00:07:51.100
For question-based analysis, check the executed query, the result period, and the cost
basis.

analytics-4
00:07:51.140 --> 00:07:54.737
This screen shows example diagnostic data.

analytics-5
00:07:54.777 --> 00:08:01.319
Do not add Codex metrics or diagnostic Traces back into completion-log usage or cost.

collector-1
00:08:02.762 --> 00:08:12.979
Once the server is ready, register a device from the Collector installation menu, then
run the authenticated dashboard’s installation command in that device’s terminal.

collector-2
00:08:13.019 --> 00:08:20.992
The device token is displayed once; store it as instructed, and do not expose its value
in a command or screen share.

collector-3
00:08:21.032 --> 00:08:27.665
In the device list, check registration details and revoke ingestion credentials when
needed.

installation-1
00:08:29.145 --> 00:08:35.862
In Installation status, check the database connection, per-client receipt status, and
schema check results.

installation-2
00:08:35.902 --> 00:08:39.900
Available menus and check scope depend on your permissions.

installation-3
00:08:39.940 --> 00:08:47.086
If you find a problem, follow the guide’s installation and operations procedures, then
check the same query interval again.

finish-1
00:08:48.479 --> 00:08:54.171
Start by aligning the period, then select users where costs or usage differ.

finish-2
00:08:54.211 --> 00:08:59.084
Follow the work, calls, and tools to find the next place to investigate.

finish-3
00:08:59.124 --> 00:09:05.796
The guide includes every menu, practical procedures, and screens you can zoom in on.

finish-4
00:09:05.836 --> 00:09:08.537
Continue exploring in whichever language you prefer.
